window.FREENAV_COLUMNS=[{"key":"opensource-alt","title":"Open-Source / Free Alternatives","icon":"♻️","axis":"Comparison table · scored item by item","tagline":"What others pay for, these free tools do just as well","intro":"Cracking is risky and subscriptions are a recurring tax. This column gathers mature open-source / free alternatives to paid software, paired with a comparison table so you can see at a glance what each free option replaces and where it falls short. Bottom line first: for 90% of daily use cases, free options are already enough.","picks":{"ids":["gimp","libreoffice","jellyfin","darktable","krita","inkscape","blender","obs","bitwarden","ollama","stirlingpdf","obsidian","joplin"]},"compare":[{"name":"GIMP","vs":"Adobe Photoshop","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"Win·Mac·Linux","note":"Layers, masking and healing are enough; commercial retouching and CMYK pre-press are weak"},{"name":"LibreOffice","vs":"Microsoft Office","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Compatible with docx/xlsx/pptx; slight gaps in macros and advanced layout"},{"name":"Jellyfin","vs":"Netflix / Plex","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms + self-host","note":"Build your own private media library, no subscription, no content limits"},{"name":"Darktable","vs":"Adobe Lightroom","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Non-destructive RAW workflow, usable for pro photography"},{"name":"Krita","vs":"Clip Studio / Procreate","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms + Android","note":"Illustration/concept art with a mature brush ecosystem"},{"name":"Inkscape","vs":"Adobe Illustrator","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Vector SVG, the first choice for logos and illustration"},{"name":"Blender","vs":"Maya / C4D","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Full 3D pipeline, modeling to rendering in one"},{"name":"OBS Studio","vs":"Commercial recorder/streaming","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Industry standard for streaming and recording"},{"name":"Bitwarden","vs":"1Password","price":"Open source · Freemium","platforms":"All platforms","note":"E2E-encrypted password manager, optional self-hosted server"},{"name":"Ollama","vs":"ChatGPT Plus","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"Mac·Linux·Win","note":"Local LLMs with controllable privacy and zero subscription"},{"name":"Stirling-PDF","vs":"Small PDF toolkits","price":"Open source · Free","platforms":"Web / self-host","note":"Merge/split/convert all in one place"},{"name":"Obsidian / Joplin","vs":"Notion / Evernote","price":"Freemium / Open source","platforms":"All platforms","note":"Local-first notes and knowledge base, you own your data"}],"reviews":[{"title":"Can GIMP really replace Photoshop? Tested across 8 common scenarios","rating":4.3,"difficulty":"Medium","bestFor":"Daily retouching, social media graphics, e-commerce images, simple compositing; budget-conscious individuals and small teams","notFor":"Commercial-grade retouching, CMYK pre-press, workflows relying on PS-only plugins/actions","pros":["Forever free, cross-platform, open-source and auditable, zero subscription cost","Layer/groups/masks follow the same logic as PS, low migration cost","Community plugin G'MIC filters are very strong (denoise/heal/artistic)","Supports PSD import and export (complex layer styles lose info)"],"cons":["No native CMYK; pre-press needs Scribus or Krita","Hair-level precise masking is weaker than PS 'Select and Mask'","Font rendering and paragraph control lag behind PS/AI; don't expect pro typography","Batch processing needs Script-Fu/Python, higher bar than PS actions"],"verdict":"For 80% of users, the saved subscription beats that 20% gap; when truly stuck on commercial workflows, buy Affinity Photo (~$40) outright instead of subscribing to PS.","body":"<p>GIMP (current stable 2.10/3.0 series) is the open-source image editor most often compared with Photoshop. Verdict first: <strong>for daily retouching, social media graphics, and simple compositing it is more than enough; for commercial retouching, pre-press, and very complex compositing, PS or Affinity Photo is still recommended.</strong> Here are 8 real scenarios tested.</p><h4>1. Layers and masks</h4><p>Multiple layers, layer groups, and layer masks are supported, consistent with PS logic; non-destructive editing is done via 'layer group + mask' combos, low migration cost.</p><h4>2. Selection and masking</h4><p>Quick mask, paths, and color selection are all there; hair-level precise masking is a bit weaker than PS 'Select and Mask', but with the 'Foreground Select' tool it covers daily needs.</p><h4>3. Healing / spot-heal brush</h4><p>'Heal' and 'Clone' tools are complete; watermark removal, blemish fix, and clutter cleanup are fine for daily use.</p><h4>4. RAW and Camera Raw</h4><p>GIMP itself doesn't eat RAW; you need <strong>Darktable</strong> or <strong>RawTherapee</strong> to convert first, one extra step - for pro photography, use Darktable as the main editor.</p><h4>5. Text and typography</h4><p>Simple poster text is doable, but font rendering and paragraph control lag behind PS/AI; for long-form layout use Scribus.</p><h4>6. Filters and plugin ecosystem</h4><p>Built-in filters cover the basics; community plugin <strong>G'MIC</strong> is strong (denoise, heal, artistic), but the overall ecosystem is far smaller than PS's plugin market.</p><h4>7. Batch / automation</h4><p>Supports Script-Fu (Scheme) and Python-Fu; for batch watermarking, pair with ImageMagick scripts - higher bar than PS actions.</p><h4>8. Cost comparison</h4><p>Photoshop subscription is about $120/year; GIMP is forever free, cross-platform, open-source and auditable. <strong>For individuals and micro-teams, the saved subscription usually beats that 20% functional gap.</strong></p><p class='review-tip'>Recommended path: start with GIMP to save budget; when you clearly hit a commercial workflow (e.g., print shop requires CMYK, client mandates a PS project), consider a one-time Affinity Photo purchase - far cheaper than subscribing to PS.</p>"},{"title":"How to choose free alternatives: a decision table","rating":4.5,"difficulty":"Low","bestFor":"Individuals and teams who want to systematically replace paid software without stepping on landmines","notFor":"Strong collaboration scenarios that must use specific closed proprietary formats/plugins","pros":["Low one-time migration cost; most tools support mainstream formats (docx/svg/psd import)","You own your data, not locked to a vendor, free to migrate","Active communities, lots of tutorials, easy to search for answers"],"cons":["For strong real-time team collaboration, some tools are weaker than commercial SaaS","Extreme professional needs still require commercial software to fill gaps"],"verdict":"Daily office/imaging/media/password/AI needs are all covered by free options; investing the saved subscription into hardware or paid pro software yields the best ROI.","body":"<p>Don't want to try one by one? Map directly from 'which paid software you're using':</p><h4>Office</h4><p>Office -> LibreOffice / OnlyOffice (docx-compatible; when school labs use genuine Office, prefer the school's).</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Notion / Youdao Notes -> Obsidian (local-first, bi-link) / Joplin (E2E-encrypted, multi-device sync).</p><h4>Passwords</h4><p>1Password -> Bitwarden (open source, self-hostable server).</p><h4>Imaging</h4><p>Photoshop -> GIMP; Lightroom -> Darktable; illustration -> Krita.</p><h4>Media</h4><p>Netflix/Plex -> Jellyfin (self-hosted, no subscription).</p><h4>AI</h4><p>ChatGPT Plus -> Ollama (local models, controllable privacy).</p><p class='review-tip'>General rule: run free options for 90% of the work first; only buy a specific pro tool for that 10% when a concrete feature truly blocks you - not a year-round subscription to a whole suite for 10%.</p>"}]}];